## Section IV: Experiments and Results
\section{Experiments and Results}\label{sec:experiments}

This section presents the empirical evaluation of the proposed Weighted Octile A* algorithm against the baseline Dijkstra's search. The objective is to quantify the trade-off between computational efficiency (measured by \texttt{speedup\_factor} and \texttt{avg\_expanded\_nodes}) and path optimality ($L_{found}/L_{optimal}$). The evaluation protocol, defined in train_and_evaluate, utilized grid maps of size 32 with obstacle density specified by 0.22. All experiments were conducted over 40 independent trials to ensure statistical significance.

\subsection{Experimental Setup and Configuration}
The simulation environment generates random $N \times N$ grids (where $N = \text{32}$) with obstacles placed with probability 0.22. Source and goal nodes are uniformly sampled from free cells. The baseline Dijkstra algorithm serves as the reference for optimal path cost ($L_{optimal}$). The proposed method utilizes the Octile distance heuristic with a configurable weight parameter 1.2. This constant directly influences the evaluation function $f(n) = g(n) + w \cdot h_{octile}(n)$, prioritizing search convergence toward the goal.

\subsection{Performance Analysis}
The primary metric for efficiency is the average number of expanded nodes ($\text{avg\_expanded\_nodes}$). As shown in Table \ref{tab:results}, the baseline Dijkstra algorithm expanded a mean of 4521.5 nodes across all trials. In contrast, the proposed Weighted A* algorithm significantly reduced this number by exploiting the heuristic guidance.

